	2. Sensing:  
Sensors: wrist RGB-D camera and the joint encoders

HSV is used because hue separates colour from brightness, so red is a thresholder band. Red wraps around hue 0, hence the two ranges (0-12 and 168-180).
		○ a cube seen from above shows its top and some side faces. Side faces are farther away, so averaging all blob pixels biases the centroid. Keeping only pixels near the nearest depth (the 10th percentile plus 6 mm) isolates the top face. The cube centre is then the top point minus half a cube height.
		○ Rejection tests (too small, touching the image border, not enough valid depth) protect against partial views, which would give a biased centroid.
	
	1. Control: The controller makes the robot’s joints follow the plan.
	- state feedback with computed torque makes the joints follow the planned trajectory

The camera finds the cube in 3D.
IK converts the grasp positions into joint angles.
The trajectory generator spreads those angles over time, so the motion is smooth.
The controller drives the joints to follow that timed plan.
